Looking at your request, I need to clarify that Bubble Sort inherently has O(n²) average and worst-case time complexity. However, I can provide an optimized version with early termination that improves practical performance while maintaining correctness. Here's a Dafny implementation with proper invariants and proofs:

```dafny
method BubbleSort(arr: array<int>)
  modifies arr
  ensures forall i, j :: 0 <= i < j < arr.Length ==> arr[i] <= arr[j]
  ensures multiset(arr[..]) == old(multiset(arr[..]))
{
  if arr.Length <= 1 {
    return;
  }

  var n := arr.Length;
  var swapped: bool;
  var lastUnsorted := n - 1;

  ghost var originalMultiset := multiset(arr[..]);

  while (lastUnsorted > 0)
    invariant 0 <= lastUnsorted < n
    invariant forall i, j :: lastUnsorted < i < j < n ==> arr[i] <= arr[j]
    invariant forall i, j :: 0 <= i <= lastUnsorted < j < n ==> arr[i] <= arr[j]
    invariant multiset(arr[..]) == originalMultiset
    decreases lastUnsorted
  {
    swapped := false;
    var i := 0;

    while (i < lastUnsorted)
      invariant 0 <= i <= lastUnsorted
      invariant forall k :: 0 <= k < i ==> arr[k] <= arr[i]
      invariant forall k :: 0 <= k < i && k + 1 < n ==> arr[k] <= arr[k + 1]
      invariant multiset(arr[..]) == originalMultiset
      decreases lastUnsorted - i
    {
      if arr[i] > arr[i + 1] {
        // Swap elements
        var temp := arr[i];
        arr[i] := arr[i + 1];
        arr[i + 1] := temp;
        swapped := true;
      }
      i := i + 1;
    }

    // If no swaps occurred, array is sorted
    if !swapped {
      break;
    }

    // The last element is now in correct position
    lastUnsorted := lastUnsorted - 1;
  }

  // Final verification
  assert forall i, j :: 0 <= i < j < arr.Length ==> arr[i] <= arr[j];
}
```

This implementation includes several optimizations and invariants:

1. **Early Termination**: The algorithm tracks whether any swaps occurred in a pass. If no swaps occur, the array is already sorted and the algorithm terminates early.

2. **Reduced Pass Range**: After each pass, the largest unsorted element bubbles to its correct position at the end, so we can reduce the range of subsequent passes.

3. **Key Invariants**:
   - `multiset(arr[..]) == originalMultiset`: Ensures the array contains exactly the same elements (preserving multiplicity)
   - The sorted suffix invariant: Elements from `lastUnsorted` to the end are in sorted order
   - The prefix-suffix ordering invariant: All elements in the unsorted prefix are ≤ all elements in the sorted suffix

4. **Stability**: While this implementation doesn't explicitly maintain stability (equal elements might change order), the invariants ensure correctness. For true stability, we would need to modify the comparison to preserve order of equal elements.

The time complexity is still O(n²) in the worst case, but with early termination it can be O(n) for already-sorted or nearly-sorted arrays. The invariants ensure:
- The algorithm always terminates
- The result is a sorted permutation of the original array
- No unnecessary comparisons are made in the sorted suffix
