Looking at the provided pseudocode, I notice it has some issues - particularly the recursive calls seem problematic (the second recursive call would cause infinite recursion). Let me provide a correct Dafny implementation that properly extracts odd numbers from an array and verifies the correctness properties.

```dafny
method extractOddNumbers(arr: array<int>) returns (result: array<int>)
  requires arr.Length > 0
  ensures forall i :: 0 <= i < result.Length ==> result[i] % 2 == 1  // All elements in result are odd
  ensures forall i :: 0 <= i < result.Length ==> exists j :: 0 <= j < arr.Length && result[i] == arr[j]  // All elements come from input
  ensures forall j :: 0 <= j < arr.Length && arr[j] % 2 == 1 ==> exists i :: 0 <= i < result.Length && result[i] == arr[j]  // All odd numbers from input are included
{
  // First, count how many odd numbers are in the array
  var count := 0;
  var index := 0;

  while index < arr.Length
    invariant 0 <= index <= arr.Length
    invariant count == |{j | 0 <= j < index && arr[j] % 2 == 1}|
  {
    if arr[index] % 2 == 1 {
      count := count + 1;
    }
    index := index + 1;
  }

  // Create result array with the exact size needed
  result := new int[count];

  // Fill the result array with odd numbers
  var resultIndex := 0;
  index := 0;

  while index < arr.Length
    invariant 0 <= index <= arr.Length
    invariant 0 <= resultIndex <= count
    invariant resultIndex == |{j | 0 <= j < index && arr[j] % 2 == 1}|
    invariant forall k :: 0 <= k < resultIndex ==> result[k] % 2 == 1
    invariant forall k :: 0 <= k < resultIndex ==> exists j :: 0 <= j < index && result[k] == arr[j]
    invariant forall j :: 0 <= j < index && arr[j] % 2 == 1 ==> exists k :: 0 <= k < resultIndex && result[k] == arr[j]
  {
    if arr[index] % 2 == 1 {
      result[resultIndex] := arr[index];
      resultIndex := resultIndex + 1;
    }
    index := index + 1;
  }
}

// Test the method
method Main() {
  var testArray := new int[5];
  testArray[0] := 1;
  testArray[1] := 2;
  testArray[2] := 3;
  testArray[3] := 4;
  testArray[4] := 5;

  var odds := extractOddNumbers(testArray);

  // Verify the result
  assert odds.Length == 3;
  assert odds[0] == 1;
  assert odds[1] == 3;
  assert odds[2] == 5;

  print "Input array: ";
  var i := 0;
  while i < testArray.Length {
    print testArray[i], " ";
    i := i + 1;
  }
  print "\n";

  print "Odd numbers: ";
  i := 0;
  while i < odds.Length {
    print odds[i], " ";
    i := i + 1;
  }
  print "\n";
}
```

This Dafny code ensures that:

1. **Only odd numbers are returned**: The postcondition `ensures forall i :: 0 <= i < result.Length ==> result[i] % 2 == 1` guarantees that every element in the result array is odd.

2. **All numbers originate from the input array**: The postcondition `ensures forall i :: 0 <= i < result.Length ==> exists j :: 0 <= j < arr.Length && result[i] == arr[j]` ensures that every element in the result comes from the input array.

3. **All odd numbers from the input are included**: The postcondition `ensures forall j :: 0 <= j < arr.Length && arr[j] % 2 == 1 ==> exists i :: 0 <= i < result.Length && result[i] == arr[j]` guarantees that every odd number from the input array appears in the result.

The method uses two passes:
- First pass: Counts how many odd numbers exist to allocate the result array with the exact size needed.
- Second pass: Copies odd numbers from the input array to the result array.

The loop invariants ensure that throughout the iteration, the partial result maintains all the required properties, allowing Dafny to verify the correctness of the implementation.
