Looking at the problem, I need to provide a complete Dafny solution for checking if a number is even. The original code has several issues including incorrect logic and non-terminating recursion. Here's a corrected version:

```dafny
method is_even(n: int) returns (result: bool)
  requires n >= 0  // Precondition: n must be non-negative
  ensures result == (n % 2 == 0)  // Postcondition: result is true iff n is even
{
  // Simple implementation using modulo operator
  result := n % 2 == 0;
}

method even_recursive(n: int) returns (result: bool)
  requires n >= 0
  ensures result == (n % 2 == 0)
  decreases n  // Termination measure
{
  if n == 0 {
    result := true;
  } else if n == 1 {
    result := false;
  } else {
    result := even_recursive(n - 2);
  }
}

method even_iterative(n: int) returns (result: bool)
  requires n >= 0
  ensures result == (n % 2 == 0)
{
  var temp := n;

  while temp > 1
    invariant temp >= 0
    invariant temp % 2 == n % 2  // Maintain parity
    decreases temp
  {
    temp := temp - 2;
  }

  result := temp == 0;
}

method Main() {
  // Test cases
  var test1 := is_even(10);
  var test2 := is_even(3);
  var test3 := even_recursive(10);
  var test4 := even_recursive(3);
  var test5 := even_iterative(10);
  var test6 := even_iterative(3);

  print "is_even(10): ", test1, "\n";
  print "is_even(3): ", test2, "\n";
  print "even_recursive(10): ", test3, "\n";
  print "even_recursive(3): ", test4, "\n";
  print "even_iterative(10): ", test5, "\n";
  print "even_iterative(3): ", test6, "\n";

  // Verification that all methods give the same result
  assert test1 == test3 && test3 == test5;
  assert test2 == test4 && test4 == test6;
}
```

This solution provides three different implementations:

1. **`is_even`**: The simplest implementation using the modulo operator
2. **`even_recursive`**: A recursive implementation that reduces the problem by 2 each time
3. **`even_iterative`**: An iterative implementation using a while loop with proper invariants

All methods include:
- Preconditions requiring non-negative input
- Postconditions specifying what the result means
- Proper termination measures (for recursive and iterative methods)
- Loop invariants for the iterative version
- Test cases in the Main method to verify correctness

The solution is efficient, correct, and Dafny-verifiable. Each method maintains the invariant that the parity (even/odd property) is preserved throughout the computation.
